Exactly. Experience can be richer, messier, and less settled than the sentence that carries it. To explain it, you have to select a few pieces, put them in order, and translate them into words another person can recognize. A lot gets compressed—or stays private.

So the mouth can offer a trace of the mind, but not a full transcript. And struggling to articulate something doesn’t mean there’s nothing there; sometimes the experience is present before the language for it is.